+### What is still a literal, and why that is safe
+
+Four kinds of value remain written here. None of them can drift silently.
+
+- **Release identities** — `0.25.0`, `v1.7.0`, `v0.5.0`, `v0.6.0`, `0.25.1`,
+  `v1.7.1`. These are the decision this plan exists to make, not a set derived
+  from something else, and each is asserted with `git describe --exact-match`
+  before use.
+- **Paths this plan owns** — `$HOME/.local/state/TASK-260830-s5ro4e/...`. No
+  production code reads them.
+- **Paths production writes as its own literals** — `$HOME/.config/task-board`
+  for the task-board install state, `$HOME/.roles`, `$HOME/.agents/skills`,
+  `$HOME/.claude/skills`, `$HOME/.codex/skills`. These are mirrored, and Steps 2
+  and 5 require `write_install_state`, `install_roles` and `register_skill`
+  byte-identical between the saved source and the release source before the
+  mirrored values are used. A release that moves one of them stops the step.
+- **The environment-override classification** — one disposition per name. The
+  list of *names* is derived; only the *decision* per name is written here, and
+  a name without a decision exits 75.
+
+The distinction that matters is fail-open versus fail-closed. A restated set
+that a release extends fails open: the extra role, skill or executable is
+installed and never rolled back, and nothing reports it. A mirrored value that a
+release changes fails closed here: the comparison is between two trees that both
+exist when the step runs, so there is no expected digest in this document to go
+stale.
+
+## task-board installer boundary: derived artifact surface
+
+Revisions 1-5 modelled the agents-infra installer's boundary and never modelled
+the task-board installer's. This section closes that, and it does so by reading
+the artifact sets out of the installer instead of restating them here.
+
+### Derived stage sequence
+
+`W2` and `W5` may not restate the stage list. It is read from the release tree
+that the step will actually execute:
+
+```bash
+extract_function_body() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  file="$1"
+  function_name="$2"
+  test -f "$file" || exit 70
+  body=""
+  if ! body="$(awk -v want="$function_name" '
+    $0 ~ "^" want "\\(\\) \\{" { inside = 1 }
+    inside                     { print }
+    inside && $0 == "}"        { exit }
+  ' "$file")"; then exit 70; fi
+  test -n "$body" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$body"
+)
+
+derive_pm_setup_files() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  source_tree="$1"
+  setup_file="$source_tree/scripts/setup.sh"
+  test -f "$setup_file" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$setup_file"
+  sourced=""
+  if ! sourced="$(sed -nE 's|^source "\$SKILL_DIR/(.*)"$|\1|p' \
+    "$setup_file")"; then exit 70; fi
+  while IFS= read -r relative; do
+    test -n "$relative" || continue
+    test -f "$source_tree/$relative" || exit 70
+    printf '%s\n' "$source_tree/$relative"
+  done <<EOF
+$sourced
+EOF
+)
+
+derive_pm_setup_stages() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  source_tree="$1"
+  setup_file="$source_tree/scripts/setup.sh"
+  setup_files=""
+  if ! setup_files="$(derive_pm_setup_files "$source_tree")"; then exit 70; fi
+
+  # Every multi-line function the installer defines, in setup.sh and in each
+  # file it sources. One-line helpers (the colour printers) are excluded by
+  # requiring the body to continue past the definition line.
+  known=""
+  while IFS= read -r file; do
+    test -n "$file" || continue
+    defined=""
+    if ! defined="$(sed -nE \
+      '/^([a-z_][a-z0-9_]*)\(\)[[:space:]]*\{.*\}[[:space:]]*$/d
+       s/^([a-z_][a-z0-9_]*)\(\)[[:space:]]*\{.*/\1/p' "$file")"; then
+      exit 70
+    fi
+    known="$known $(printf '%s' "$defined" | tr '\n' ' ')"
+  done <<EOF
+$setup_files
+EOF
+  test -n "$known" || exit 70
+
+  body=""
+  if ! body="$(extract_function_body "$setup_file" setup_main)"; then exit 70; fi
+  stages=""
+  if ! stages="$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | awk -v known="$known" '
+    BEGIN {
+      n = split(known, names, " ")
+      for (i = 1; i <= n; i++)
+        if (names[i] != "" && names[i] != "setup_main") is_stage[names[i]] = 1
+    }
+    {
+      for (name in is_stage)
+        if ($0 ~ ("(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])" name "([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)"))
+          if (!(name in seen)) { seen[name] = 1; order[++count] = name }
+    }
+    END { for (i = 1; i <= count; i++) print order[i] }
+  ')"; then exit 70; fi
+  test -n "$stages" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$stages"
+)
+```
+
+Executed against the installed task-board source at planning time, this derives
+sixteen stages in `setup_main` order:
+
+```text
+parse_args install_go build_cli build_sessiond build_tui install_binary
+register_skill install_roles write_install_state check_path configure_project
+check_agents_infra_compose install_skills verify bootstrap_local_agents
+print_local_agents_hint
+```
+
+`configure_project`, `check_agents_infra_compose`, `bootstrap_local_agents` and
+`print_local_agents_hint` return immediately or are not reached when
+`CONFIGURE_PROJECT` is `0`, which the mandatory bare invocation in Steps 2 and 5
+guarantees. **`install_skills` is not one of them.** It runs unconditionally,
+between `check_agents_infra_compose` and `verify`, on every bare machine-scoped
+install. Revisions 1-5 asserted a `W2`/`W5` artifact list that ended at "roles
+and install state" and therefore excluded a stage that the installer always
+runs.
+
+### What `install_skills` does, and to what
+
+`install_skills` iterates the `SKILL_REPOS` map. For each entry it selects one
+of two branches on `~/.agents/skills/<skill>`:
+
+- **already-installed branch** — the path is a real directory and not a
+  symlink. Nothing is cloned. `scrub_git_metadata` then removes `.git`,
+  `.gitignore`, `.gitattributes` and `.gitmodules` from the installed tree if
+  present, and both `~/.claude/skills/<skill>` and `~/.codex/skills/<skill>`
+  are removed and recreated as symlinks (`rm -rf` when they are real
+  directories).
+- **clone branch** — the path is absent or a symlink. The symlink is removed
+  first, then `git clone` runs against a remote GitHub/SSH URL. **A failed
+  clone hits `continue`, which leaves the skill removed** and, on the next
+  loop iteration for that skill, nothing to relink.
+
+The clone branch reaches the network from inside the release window, installs an
+unrelated skill at whatever its remote HEAD happens to be, and its failure mode
+deletes an artifact. Neither belongs in a lockstep contract migration.
+
+### Measured host state of that surface
+
+Read-only measurement of the eight `SKILL_REPOS` entries, taken at planning
+time on the target host. Each row is a completed read; a failed read would be
+reported as `unknown` and is not present here.
+
+| Skill | `~/.agents/skills/<s>` | git metadata | `~/.claude/skills/<s>` | `~/.codex/skills/<s>` | Branch selected |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `agent-facing-api` | real directory | absent | symlink to the agents copy | symlink to the agents copy | already-installed |
+| `android-testing-tools` | real directory | absent | symlink to the agents copy | symlink to the agents copy | already-installed |
+| `architecture-diagrams` | real directory | absent | symlink to the agents copy | symlink to the agents copy | already-installed |
+| `go-testing-tools` | real directory | absent | symlink to the agents copy | symlink to the agents copy | already-installed |
+| `ios-testing-tools` | real directory | absent | symlink to the agents copy | symlink to the agents copy | already-installed |
+| `product-appraisal` | real directory | absent | symlink to the agents copy | symlink to the agents copy | already-installed |
+| `core-data` | real directory | absent | symlink to the agents copy | symlink to the agents copy | already-installed |
+| `swiftui` | real directory | absent | symlink to the agents copy | symlink to the agents copy | already-installed |
+
+`core-data` and `swiftui` additionally carry a root `SKILL.md` symlink into
+their nested skill directory, so the `SKILL_NESTED` branch is also a measured
+no-op today. Combined size of the eight trees is roughly 3 MB, which is why
+snapshotting them is affordable rather than a reason to leave them unmodelled.
+
+On this host today the destructive branch is therefore **not** selected for any
+of the eight, and the residual mutation of a bare install is sixteen recreated
+symlinks pointing at their existing targets. That is a measurement of one host
+at one time. It is not a property of the plan, and the plan does not assume it
+will still hold when the release runs.
+
+### How Steps 2 and 5 handle it
+
+Three mechanisms, in order:
+
+1. **Precondition, outside the window.** Before the install command runs, every
+   skill the release's own `SKILL_REPOS` names must already be a real directory.
+   This converts the clone branch into the already-installed branch *before*
+   the release window opens, so no network clone and no delete-on-failure can
+   happen inside it. A skill that is absent or a symlink is materialised by the
+   operator beforehand, from its own repository, under their own control and
+   with their own SSH agent — not by the release installer in a non-interactive
+   shell.
+2. **Snapshot.** The eight trees and their sixteen link states are part of the
+   `current-pair` and `bridge-pair` snapshots, so the residual mutations
+   (`scrub_git_metadata`, symlink recreation) and any branch the precondition
+   did not foresee are recoverable.
+3. **Restore.** `restore_project_management_surface` returns each managed skill
+   tree and both link trees to their snapshot state, and removes a managed
+   skill the release created that the snapshot did not contain.
+
+```bash
+derive_pm_managed_skill_names() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  source_tree="$1"
+  setup_file="$source_tree/scripts/setup.sh"
+  test -f "$setup_file" || exit 70
+  # register_skill installs exactly one skill and names it in one assignment.
+  primary=""
+  if ! primary="$(sed -nE \
+    's/^[[:space:]]*local skill_name="([^"]+)".*/\1/p' "$setup_file")"; then
+    exit 70
+  fi
+  if ! test "$(printf '%s\n' "$primary" | awk \
+    'NF { count++ } END { print count + 0 }')" -eq 1; then exit 70; fi
+  # install_skills iterates the SKILL_REPOS map; its keys are every dependency
+  # skill the same run installs, clones, scrubs or relinks.
+  dependencies=""
+  if ! dependencies="$(awk '
+    /^SKILL_REPOS=\(/ { inside = 1; next }
+    inside && /^\)/   { inside = 0 }
+    inside && NF >= 2 { print $1 }
+  ' "$setup_file")"; then exit 70; fi
+  test -n "$dependencies" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$primary"
+  printf '%s\n' "$dependencies"
+)
+
+require_dependency_skills_materialized() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  release_source="$1"
+  skills=""
+  if ! skills="$(derive_pm_managed_skill_names "$release_source")"; then
+    exit 70
+  fi
+  refused=0
+  while IFS= read -r skill; do
+    test -n "$skill" || continue
+    target="$HOME/.agents/skills/$skill"
+    if test -L "$target"; then
+      printf 'REFUSE|%s|symlink; installer would remove it and clone\n' "$skill"
+      refused=1
+    elif test -d "$target"; then
+      printf 'OK|%s|real directory; already-installed branch\n' "$skill"
+    elif test -e "$target"; then
+      printf 'REFUSE|%s|present but not a directory\n' "$skill"
+      refused=1
+    else
+      printf 'REFUSE|%s|absent; installer would clone from the network\n' "$skill"
+      refused=1
+    fi
+  done <<EOF
+$skills
+EOF
+  test "$refused" -eq 0 || exit 74
+)
+```
+
+The precondition is derived from the release's own map, so a ninth skill added
+by `0.25.0` or `0.25.1` is covered without editing this document. A read that
+fails — an unreadable `scripts/setup.sh`, an empty derived map — exits non-zero
+rather than reporting an empty, satisfied set.
+
+### Mirror guard
+
+Two behaviours above are mirrored logic rather than derived data: this document
+describes what `install_skills` does with each branch, and it reproduces the
+installer's config-directory rule for agents-infra. Mirrored logic that the
+release silently changes is the same defect class as a stale literal, so both
+are compared between the installed pair's source and the release candidate's
+source at execution time. There is no expected digest written here to go stale;
+the comparison is between two trees that both exist when the step runs.
+
+```bash
+require_mirrored_function_unchanged() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  baseline_file="$1"
+  candidate_file="$2"
+  function_name="$3"
+  baseline=""
+  candidate=""
+  if ! baseline="$(extract_function_body "$baseline_file" \
+    "$function_name")"; then exit 70; fi
+  if ! candidate="$(extract_function_body "$candidate_file" \
+    "$function_name")"; then exit 70; fi
+  if ! test "$baseline" = "$candidate"; then exit 73; fi
+  printf 'MIRROR_UNCHANGED|%s\n' "$function_name"
+)
+```
+
+Steps 2 and 5 require `install_skills` unchanged between the saved source and
+the release source. Steps 3 and 6 require `resolve_config_dir` and
+`install_lldb_mcp` unchanged. A changed body stops the step and sends the
+operator back to re-derive this section before releasing, which is the intended
+outcome: a release that changes the installer's boundary needs a reviewed plan
+that describes the new boundary.
+
+## Recovery snapshot and restore primitives
+
+Run this before Step 2 and repeat into a new `bridge-pair` snapshot after both
+`0.25.0` and `v1.7.0` pass installed canaries. These commands copy no
+credential, cookie, token, keychain data or environment value.
+
+Every artifact set below is derived from the artifact that owns it. Nothing in
+this section names a binary, a role, a skill, a repository path or an install
+directory that the plan itself decided. The rule this revision enforces is:
+**if production reads a set at run time, or a release owns the literal that
+defines it, this document derives it too.** A set restated here is a set that
+stops matching the next release without anything failing.
+
+### Derivation helpers
+
+```bash
+set -euo pipefail
+
+read_install_state_field() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  state_file="$1"
+  field="$2"
+  test -f "$state_file" || exit 70
+  value=""
+  if ! value="$(python3 - "$state_file" "$field" <<'PY'
+import json, sys
+try:
+    with open(sys.argv[1]) as handle:
+        state = json.load(handle)
+except Exception:
+    sys.exit(70)
+value = state.get(sys.argv[2])
+if not isinstance(value, str) or value == "":
+    sys.exit(71)
+sys.stdout.write(value)
+PY
+  )"; then exit 70; fi
+  test -n "$value" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$value"
+)
+
+derive_pm_binary_names() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  source_tree="$1"
+  setup_file="$source_tree/scripts/setup.sh"
+  test -f "$setup_file" || exit 70
+  # setup_main names each managed executable in its own install_binary call.
+  names=""
+  if ! names="$(sed -nE 's/^[[:space:]]*install_binary "([^"]+)".*/\1/p' \
+    "$setup_file")"; then exit 70; fi
+  test -n "$names" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$names"
+)
+
+derive_ai_binary_names() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  source_tree="$1"
+  setup_file="$source_tree/scripts/setup.sh"
+  test -f "$setup_file" || exit 70
+  for variable in BINARY_NAME MODEL_HARNESS_BINARY_NAME; do
+    value=""
+    if ! value="$(sed -nE "s/^$variable=\"([^\"]+)\".*/\1/p" \
+      "$setup_file")"; then exit 70; fi
+    if ! test "$(printf '%s\n' "$value" | awk \
+      'NF { count++ } END { print count + 0 }')" -eq 1; then exit 70; fi
+    printf '%s\n' "$value"
+  done
+)
+
+derive_pm_role_names() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  source_tree="$1"
+  roles_dir="$source_tree/.roles"
+  # install_roles iterates "$SKILL_DIR/.roles"/* and installs whatever is
+  # there. A role added by a release is therefore installed by that release and
+  # must be visible to the rollback the same way.
+  test -d "$roles_dir" || exit 70
+  names=""
+  if ! names="$(ls -1A "$roles_dir")"; then exit 70; fi
+  test -n "$names" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$names"
+)
+
+derive_ai_config_dir() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  # Mirrors resolve_config_dir. The mirror is not trusted on its own: Steps 3
+  # and 6 require that function byte-identical between the saved source and the
+  # release source before this value is used.
+  if test -n "${AGENTS_INFRA_CONFIG_DIR:-}"; then
+    printf '%s\n' "$AGENTS_INFRA_CONFIG_DIR"
+    exit 0
+  fi
+  kernel=""
+  if ! kernel="$(uname -s)"; then exit 70; fi
+  if test "$kernel" = Darwin; then
+    printf '%s/Library/Application Support/agents-infra\n' "$HOME"
+    exit 0
+  fi
+  printf '%s/agents-infra\n' "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}"
+)
+
+derive_setup_env_override_names() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  setup_file="$1"
+  test -f "$setup_file" || exit 70
+  names=""
+  if ! names="$(grep -oE '\$\{[A-Z_]+:-' "$setup_file" \
+    | sed -E 's/\$\{([A-Z_]+):-/\1/' | sort -u)"; then exit 70; fi
+  test -n "$names" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$names"
+)
+
+classify_pm_env_override() {
+  case "$1" in
+    TASK_BOARD_PROJECT_DIR|TASK_BOARD_CONFIGURE_PROJECT|TASK_BOARD_MODE|\
+    TASK_BOARD_DIR|TASK_BOARD_REMOTE|TASK_BOARD_USER|TASK_BOARD_ID|\
+    TASK_BOARD_TLS_NO_VERIFY|TASK_BOARD_BOOTSTRAP_LOCAL_AGENTS|\
+    TASK_BOARD_SETUP_SOURCE_ONLY)
+      printf 'unset\n' ;;
+    TMPDIR)
+      printf 'keep\n' ;;
+    *)
+      return 1 ;;
+  esac
+}
+
+classify_ai_env_override() {
+  case "$1" in
+    BIN_DIR)
+      printf 'unset\n' ;;
+    AGENTS_INFRA_SKIP_LLDB_MCP)
+      printf 'force-1\n' ;;
+    AGENTS_INFRA_CONFIG_DIR|HOME|XDG_CONFIG_HOME)
+      printf 'keep\n' ;;
+    *)
+      return 1 ;;
+  esac
+}
+
+require_env_overrides_classified() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  setup_file="$1"
+  classifier="$2"
+  names=""
+  if ! names="$(derive_setup_env_override_names "$setup_file")"; then exit 70; fi
+  unclassified=0
+  while IFS= read -r name; do
+    test -n "$name" || continue
+    disposition=""
+    if ! disposition="$("$classifier" "$name")"; then
+      printf 'UNCLASSIFIED|%s\n' "$name"
+      unclassified=1
+      continue
+    fi
+    printf '%s|%s\n' "$disposition" "$name"
+  done <<EOF
+$names
+EOF
+  test "$unclassified" -eq 0 || exit 75
+)
+```
+
+An environment override that a release adds and this plan has not classified
+makes `require_env_overrides_classified` exit 75. The step stops rather than
+running an installer whose behaviour is partly controlled by an input nobody
+decided about. That is the same fail-closed shape as the derived skill and role
+sets: the plan does not carry the list, it carries a decision for every member
+of a list it reads.
+
+### Identity of the saved pair
+
+```bash
+extract_reported_commit() {
+  version_text="$1"
+  parsed="$(printf '%s\n' "$version_text" | sed -nE \
+    -e 's/.*\(commit ([0-9a-f]{7,40}),.*/\1/p' \
+    -e 's/.* commit=([0-9a-f]{7,40})( .*)?$/\1/p')"
+  if ! test -n "$parsed"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! test "$(printf '%s\n' "$parsed" | awk \
+    'NF { count++ } END { print count + 0 }')" -eq 1; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  printf '%s\n' "$parsed"
+}
+
+require_saved_binary_source_identity() {
+  binary_name="$1"
+  source_tree="$2"
+  version_text="$3"
+  if ! source_head="$(git -C "$source_tree" rev-parse HEAD)"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! reported_commit="$(extract_reported_commit "$version_text")"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! reported_head="$(git -C "$source_tree" \
+    rev-parse "$reported_commit^{commit}")"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! test -z "$(git -C "$source_tree" status --porcelain)"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! test "$reported_head" = "$source_head"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  printf '%s source_head=%s reported_commit=%s\n' \
+    "$binary_name" "$source_head" "$reported_commit"
+}
+
+# The recovery source is derived from the saved executable, never from a
+# literal revision written into this document. A pinned commit is stale the
+# moment either repository moves; this resolves the pair at execution time and
+# refuses when the installed artifact cannot be resolved to a commit.
+materialize_recovery_source() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  repo="$1"
+  destination="$2"
+  version_text="$3"
+
+  reported_commit=""
+  if ! reported_commit="$(extract_reported_commit "$version_text")"; then
+    exit 1
+  fi
+  if test -z "$reported_commit"; then exit 1; fi
+
+  resolved=""
+  if ! resolved="$(git -C "$repo" rev-parse --verify --quiet \
+    "${reported_commit}^{commit}")"; then
+    exit 1
+  fi
+  if test -z "$resolved"; then exit 1; fi
+
+  if ! git -C "$repo" worktree add --detach "$destination" "$resolved" >&2; then
+    exit 1
+  fi
+  # `git -C repo worktree add` resolves a relative destination against the
+  # repository, not the caller's directory, and still exits zero. Publishing the
+  # OID for a worktree that is not where the caller asked is a failed read
+  # wearing the shape of a success, so the destination is confirmed to exist
+  # before the commit is reported.
+  if ! test -e "$destination/.git"; then
+    exit 1
+  fi
+  printf '%s\n' "$resolved"
+)
+```
+
+### Snapshot
+
+```bash
+set -euo pipefail
+
+# The repositories and the install directory are read out of the install state
+# the installers themselves wrote. No host path is decided by this document.
+PM_STATE="$HOME/.config/task-board/install.json"
+AI_STATE="$(derive_ai_config_dir)/install.json"
+PM_REPO="$(read_install_state_field "$PM_STATE" repoPath)"
+AI_REPO="$(read_install_state_field "$AI_STATE" repoPath)"
+PM_BIN_DIR="$(read_install_state_field "$PM_STATE" binDir)"
+AI_BIN_DIR="$(read_install_state_field "$AI_STATE" binDir)"
+test "$PM_BIN_DIR" = "$AI_BIN_DIR"
+BIN_DIR="$PM_BIN_DIR"
+test -d "$PM_REPO"
+test -d "$AI_REPO"
+
+RECOVERY_ROOT="$HOME/.local/state/TASK-260830-s5ro4e/current-pair"
+RECOVERY_BIN="$RECOVERY_ROOT/bin"
+MANIFEST="$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest"
+
+test ! -e "$RECOVERY_ROOT"
+mkdir -p "$RECOVERY_BIN" "$MANIFEST" "$RECOVERY_ROOT/pm/roles" \
+  "$RECOVERY_ROOT/pm/skills" "$RECOVERY_ROOT/ai" "$RECOVERY_ROOT/sources"
+
+printf '%s\n' "$BIN_DIR"  > "$MANIFEST/bin-dir.txt"
+printf '%s\n' "$PM_REPO"  > "$MANIFEST/pm-repo.txt"
+printf '%s\n' "$AI_REPO"  > "$MANIFEST/ai-repo.txt"
+printf '%s\n' "$PM_STATE" > "$MANIFEST/pm-state-path.txt"
+printf '%s\n' "$AI_STATE" > "$MANIFEST/ai-state-path.txt"
+
+# Every directory the restore writes into must share a filesystem with the
+# snapshot, so a restore is an atomic same-device rename rather than a copy that
+# can fail half way.
+RECOVERY_DEVICE="$(stat -f %d "$RECOVERY_ROOT")"
+for target in "$BIN_DIR" "$HOME/.agents/skills" "$HOME/.roles" \
+  "$HOME/.claude/skills" "$HOME/.codex/skills" \
+  "$(dirname "$PM_STATE")" "$(dirname "$AI_STATE")"; do
+  test -d "$target"
+  test "$(stat -f %d "$target")" = "$RECOVERY_DEVICE"
+done
+
+# Bootstrap the managed executable set from the checked-out repositories, copy
+# it, read the versions it reports, then materialise each recovery source at
+# exactly the commit its own saved executable names.
+bootstrap_names="$(derive_pm_binary_names "$PM_REPO"
+derive_ai_binary_names "$AI_REPO")"
+while IFS= read -r name; do
+  test -n "$name" || continue
+  test -f "$BIN_DIR/$name"
+  test ! -L "$BIN_DIR/$name"
+  cp -p "$BIN_DIR/$name" "$RECOVERY_BIN/$name"
+  cmp -s "$BIN_DIR/$name" "$RECOVERY_BIN/$name"
+done <<EOF
+$bootstrap_names
+EOF
+
+TB_VERSION="$("$RECOVERY_BIN/task-board" --version)"
+SESSIOND_VERSION="$("$RECOVERY_BIN/tb-sessiond" --version)"
+AI_VERSION="$("$RECOVERY_BIN/agents-infra" version)"
+printf '%s\n%s\n%s\n' "$TB_VERSION" "$SESSIOND_VERSION" "$AI_VERSION" \
+  > "$RECOVERY_ROOT/versions.txt"
+
+TB_SOURCE="$RECOVERY_ROOT/sources/skill-project-management"
+AI_SOURCE="$RECOVERY_ROOT/sources/relux-agents-infra"
+TB_COMMIT="$(materialize_recovery_source "$PM_REPO" "$TB_SOURCE" "$TB_VERSION")"
+AI_COMMIT="$(materialize_recovery_source "$AI_REPO" "$AI_SOURCE" "$AI_VERSION")"
+
+# task-board and tb-sessiond ship from one repository and share one recovery
+# source worktree. A divergent reported commit means the installed pair is not
+# coherent, and it blocks Step 2 rather than picking one of the two.
+test "$(extract_reported_commit "$SESSIOND_VERSION")" \
+  = "$(extract_reported_commit "$TB_VERSION")"
+
+# Re-derive the managed executable set from the exact sources the installed
+# binaries were built from. If a repository HEAD moved and changed that set, the
+# bootstrap copy is not the installed release's artifact set, and Step 2 stops.
+managed_names="$(derive_pm_binary_names "$TB_SOURCE"
+derive_ai_binary_names "$AI_SOURCE")"
+test "$managed_names" = "$bootstrap_names"
+printf '%s\n' "$managed_names" > "$MANIFEST/binaries.txt"
+
+# The complete install-directory listing is kept so a rollback can report any
+# other entry that appeared during the window, even one this plan does not own.
+ls -1A "$BIN_DIR" > "$MANIFEST/bin-inventory.txt"
+shasum -a 256 "$RECOVERY_BIN"/* > "$RECOVERY_ROOT/bin.sha256"
+
+printf 'skill-project-management=%s\nrelux-agents-infra=%s\n' \
+  "$TB_COMMIT" "$AI_COMMIT" > "$RECOVERY_ROOT/recovery-source-commits.txt"
+{
+  require_saved_binary_source_identity task-board "$TB_SOURCE" "$TB_VERSION"
+  require_saved_binary_source_identity tb-sessiond "$TB_SOURCE" \
+    "$SESSIOND_VERSION"
+  require_saved_binary_source_identity agents-infra "$AI_SOURCE" "$AI_VERSION"
+} > "$RECOVERY_ROOT/binary-source-identities.txt"
+
+# Roles: the whole installed role surface is saved and its names recorded. The
+# rollback compares that recorded set against the role set the release actually
+# owns, so a role a release adds is removed again on rollback.
+ls -1A "$HOME/.roles" > "$MANIFEST/roles.txt"
+while IFS= read -r role; do
+  test -n "$role" || continue
+  test -d "$HOME/.roles/$role"
+  rsync -a --delete "$HOME/.roles/$role/" "$RECOVERY_ROOT/pm/roles/$role/"
+done < "$MANIFEST/roles.txt"
+
+# Skills: every skill the installer manages, derived from the installer itself.
+# This covers the registered project-management copy and every SKILL_REPOS
+# dependency that install_skills clones, scrubs or relinks.
+derive_pm_managed_skill_names "$TB_SOURCE" > "$MANIFEST/skills.txt"
+: > "$MANIFEST/skill-state.txt"
+while IFS= read -r skill; do
+  test -n "$skill" || continue
+  case "$skill" in *"|"*) exit 76 ;; esac
+  agents_target="$HOME/.agents/skills/$skill"
+
+  if test -L "$agents_target"; then
+    agents_kind=symlink
+    agents_link="$(readlink "$agents_target")"
+  elif test -d "$agents_target"; then
+    agents_kind=directory
+    agents_link=""
+    rsync -a --delete "$agents_target/" "$RECOVERY_ROOT/pm/skills/$skill/"
+  elif test -e "$agents_target"; then
+    exit 77
+  else
+    agents_kind=absent
+    agents_link=""
+  fi
+
+  line="$skill|$agents_kind|$agents_link"
+  for link_root in "$HOME/.claude/skills" "$HOME/.codex/skills"; do
+    link_target="$link_root/$skill"
+    if test -L "$link_target"; then
+      line="$line|symlink|$(readlink "$link_target")"
+    elif test -d "$link_target"; then
+      line="$line|directory|"
+    elif test -e "$link_target"; then
+      exit 77
+    else
+      line="$line|absent|"
+    fi
+  done
+  printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$MANIFEST/skill-state.txt"
+done < "$MANIFEST/skills.txt"
+
+# Both installers' machine-scoped install state. The agents-infra one records
+# repoPath, which the installed binary uses to resolve its own source tree, so a
+# rollback that leaves it naming an abandoned release candidate leaves a binary
+# that cannot find its source.
+install -m 0644 "$PM_STATE" "$RECOVERY_ROOT/pm/install.json"
+install -m 0644 "$AI_STATE" "$RECOVERY_ROOT/ai/install.json"
+```
+
+Parse failure, an unknown commit, dirty detached source, a binary/source OID
+mismatch, a failed `cmp`, a derived set that changed between the bootstrap and
+the exact source, or any missing executable blocks Step 2. None of them is
+treated as an absent identity or a usable rollback baseline.
+
+**Property of this plan: the recovery baseline and the recovery binary are the
+same pair by construction, not by a maintained literal.** Each source worktree
+is created at the commit its own saved executable reports, resolved in the
+owning repository at execution time. There is no revision, executable name,
+role name, skill name, repository path or install directory written into this
+document that has to be refreshed when either repository moves. If an
+executable's version output cannot be parsed to a commit, or that commit does
+not resolve in the repository, `materialize_recovery_source` refuses and no
+baseline is produced — the plan reports `unknown` and stops rather than
+guessing a nearby revision. `require_saved_binary_source_identity` therefore
+becomes a tautology guard that still catches a concurrent repository mutation
+or a tampered worktree between materialization and use.
+
+For the bridge snapshot, use a fresh
+`$HOME/.local/state/TASK-260830-s5ro4e/bridge-pair`. The same derivation applies
+unchanged: the saved `0.25.0` and `v1.7.0` executables name their own commits,
+their sources name their own artifact sets, and the exact tags are asserted
+against the resolved OIDs rather than substituted for them. Never overwrite a
+prior snapshot.
+
+### Restore primitives
+
+Each rollback block below runs in one `set -euo pipefail` shell after defining
+the primitives exactly as written above and here. A partial primitive
+invocation is a rollback failure, not a successful half-restore. Every restore
+takes the release source tree it is rolling back, because the set of artifacts a
+release *added* is derivable only from that release.
+
+```bash
+snapshot_bin_dir() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  test -f "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/bin-dir.txt" || exit 70
+  value="$(cat "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/bin-dir.txt")"
+  test -n "$value" || exit 70
+  printf '%s\n' "$value"
+)
+
+restore_binary() {
+  name="$1"
+  bin_dir="$(snapshot_bin_dir)"
+  source_file="$RECOVERY_ROOT/bin/$name"
+  destination="$bin_dir/$name"
+  temporary="$bin_dir/.$name.rollback.$$"
+  test -f "$source_file"
+  test ! -L "$source_file"
+  install -m 0755 "$source_file" "$temporary"
+  mv -f "$temporary" "$destination"
+}
+
+# Restore exactly the executables the snapshot holds for one repository, and
+# quarantine any executable that repository's release added and the snapshot
+# does not have. Both sets are derived: the first from the snapshot manifest,
+# the second from the release tree that was installed.
+restore_binaries_for_release() {
+  derive_names="$1"
+  release_source="$2"
+  bin_dir="$(snapshot_bin_dir)"
+
+  release_names="$("$derive_names" "$release_source")"
+  snapshot_names="$(cat "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/binaries.txt")"
+  test -n "$release_names"
+  test -n "$snapshot_names"
+
+  while IFS= read -r name; do
+    test -n "$name" || continue
+    if printf '%s\n' "$snapshot_names" | grep -qxF "$name"; then
+      restore_binary "$name"
+    elif test -e "$bin_dir/$name"; then
+      mv -f "$bin_dir/$name" "$bin_dir/.$name.superseded.$$"
+    fi
+  done <<EOF
+$release_names
+EOF
+
+  # Anything else that appeared in the install directory during the window is
+  # reported, not silently removed: this plan owns the release's artifacts, not
+  # the operator's whole PATH.
+  ls -1A "$bin_dir" \
+    | grep -vxF -f "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/bin-inventory.txt" \
+    > "$RECOVERY_ROOT/unexpected-bin-entries.txt" || true
+}
+
+restore_roles_from_snapshot() {
+  release_source="$1"
+  snapshot_roles="$(cat "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/roles.txt")"
+  release_roles="$(derive_pm_role_names "$release_source")"
+  test -n "$snapshot_roles"
+  test -n "$release_roles"
+
+  while IFS= read -r role; do
+    test -n "$role" || continue
+    stage="$HOME/.roles/.$role.rollback.$$"
+    rsync -a --delete "$RECOVERY_ROOT/pm/roles/$role/" "$stage/"
+    if test -e "$HOME/.roles/$role"; then
+      mv "$HOME/.roles/$role" "$HOME/.roles/.$role.failed.$$"
+    fi
+    mv "$stage" "$HOME/.roles/$role"
+  done <<EOF
+$snapshot_roles
+EOF
+
+  # A role the release added is not part of the previous working state.
+  while IFS= read -r role; do
+    test -n "$role" || continue
+    if printf '%s\n' "$snapshot_roles" | grep -qxF "$role"; then
+      continue
+    fi
+    if test -e "$HOME/.roles/$role"; then
+      mv "$HOME/.roles/$role" "$HOME/.roles/.$role.superseded.$$"
+    fi
+  done <<EOF
+$release_roles
+EOF
+}
+
+restore_skill_entry() {
+  entry_path="$1"
+  entry_kind="$2"
+  entry_link="$3"
+  staged_tree="$4"
+  entry_dir="$(dirname "$entry_path")"
+  entry_name="$(basename "$entry_path")"
+
+  case "$entry_kind" in
+    symlink)
+      rm -rf "$entry_path"
+      ln -sfn "$entry_link" "$entry_path"
+      ;;
+    directory)
+      test -n "$staged_tree"
+      test -d "$staged_tree"
+      stage="$entry_dir/.$entry_name.rollback.$$"
+      rsync -a --delete "$staged_tree/" "$stage/"
+      if test -e "$entry_path" || test -L "$entry_path"; then
+        mv "$entry_path" "$entry_dir/.$entry_name.failed.$$"
+      fi
+      mv "$stage" "$entry_path"
+      ;;
+    absent)
+      if test -e "$entry_path" || test -L "$entry_path"; then
+        mv "$entry_path" "$entry_dir/.$entry_name.superseded.$$"
+      fi
+      ;;
+    *)
+      return 1
+      ;;
+  esac
+}
+
+restore_managed_skills_from_snapshot() {
+  release_source="$1"
+  snapshot_skills="$(cat "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/skills.txt")"
+  release_skills="$(derive_pm_managed_skill_names "$release_source")"
+  test -n "$snapshot_skills"
+  test -n "$release_skills"
+
+  while IFS= read -r line; do
+    test -n "$line" || continue
+    skill="$(printf '%s' "$line" | cut -d'|' -f1)"
+    agents_kind="$(printf '%s' "$line" | cut -d'|' -f2)"
+    agents_link="$(printf '%s' "$line" | cut -d'|' -f3)"
+    claude_kind="$(printf '%s' "$line" | cut -d'|' -f4)"
+    claude_link="$(printf '%s' "$line" | cut -d'|' -f5)"
+    codex_kind="$(printf '%s' "$line" | cut -d'|' -f6)"
+    codex_link="$(printf '%s' "$line" | cut -d'|' -f7)"
+
+    restore_skill_entry "$HOME/.agents/skills/$skill" "$agents_kind" \
+      "$agents_link" "$RECOVERY_ROOT/pm/skills/$skill"
+    restore_skill_entry "$HOME/.claude/skills/$skill" "$claude_kind" \
+      "$claude_link" ""
+    restore_skill_entry "$HOME/.codex/skills/$skill" "$codex_kind" \
+      "$codex_link" ""
+  done < "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/skill-state.txt"
+
+  # A skill the release's SKILL_REPOS added is not part of the previous state.
+  while IFS= read -r skill; do
+    test -n "$skill" || continue
+    if printf '%s\n' "$snapshot_skills" | grep -qxF "$skill"; then
+      continue
+    fi
+    restore_skill_entry "$HOME/.agents/skills/$skill" absent "" ""
+    restore_skill_entry "$HOME/.claude/skills/$skill" absent "" ""
+    restore_skill_entry "$HOME/.codex/skills/$skill" absent "" ""
+  done <<EOF
+$release_skills
+EOF
+}
+
+restore_install_state() {
+  saved="$1"
+  destination="$2"
+  temporary="$(dirname "$destination")/.$(basename "$destination").rollback.$$"
+  test -f "$saved"
+  install -m 0644 "$saved" "$temporary"
+  mv -f "$temporary" "$destination"
+}
+
+# Coherent task-board skill/role/binary restore, executed only for the
+# release-owned artifacts derived above.
+restore_project_management_surface() {
+  release_source="$1"
+  restore_binaries_for_release derive_pm_binary_names "$release_source"
+  restore_managed_skills_from_snapshot "$release_source"
+  restore_roles_from_snapshot "$release_source"
+  restore_install_state "$RECOVERY_ROOT/pm/install.json" \
+    "$(cat "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/pm-state-path.txt")"
+}
+
+# Coherent agents-infra managed-runtime restore uses the saved old executables
+# and exact old source. It deliberately does not copy `~/.agents` wholesale and
+# does not print config contents. The install state is restored last because the
+# installed binary resolves its own source tree through that file.
+restore_agents_infra_surface() {
+  release_source="$1"
+  restore_binaries_for_release derive_ai_binary_names "$release_source"
+  "$RECOVERY_ROOT/bin/agents-infra" setup global \
+    --source-dir "$RECOVERY_ROOT/sources/relux-agents-infra"
+  "$RECOVERY_ROOT/bin/agents-infra" verify global
+  restore_install_state "$RECOVERY_ROOT/ai/install.json" \
+    "$(cat "$RECOVERY_ROOT/manifest/ai-state-path.txt")"
+}
+```
+
+The restored agents-infra install state names the original repository path,
+while the runtime receipt written by the restore names the recovery source
+worktree. That divergence is deliberate and must be recorded with the rollback
+evidence: the recovery source worktree is retained until a clean reinstall from
+the original repository re-mints both. Deleting `$RECOVERY_ROOT` after a
+rollback leaves a runtime whose receipt names a path that no longer exists.
+
+All restore procedures in this plan are **UNTESTED operationally** until a
+release operator rehearses them on a disposable home/runtime and records the
+real exits. They are not assumed reversible. The construction above has been
+attacked in a disposable sandbox HOME (see "Validation evidence"), which shows
+the derivations and the removal branches behave as written; that is not a
+substitute for an operational rehearsal against a real installed pair.
+
+## Release order
+
+### Step 0 — freeze the current working pair
+
+Repository/version: whatever pair is actually installed when this step runs.
+The operator does not read a version out of this document. The snapshot block
+above reads `task-board --version`, `tb-sessiond --version` and
+`agents-infra version` from the *saved* executables and materializes each
+recovery source at the commit those executables report.
+
+At the time this plan was written the installed pair measured
+`0.24.3-172-g063197b1` embedding `v0.2.0` and `v1.6.1-103-g4270549`. That is
+recorded as an observation, not as a pin: both repositories moved during this
+planning task alone, so any literal here is expected to be stale by the time
+the migration runs, and nothing in the procedure depends on these values.
+
+Required board capability: run read, preflight, one real spawn, outcome
+resource handoff and reviewer route using the installed pair, then capture the
+`current-pair` recovery snapshot above. The snapshot is admissible only when
+its task-board, tb-sessiond and agents-infra version outputs each resolve to the
+HEAD of their saved clean detached source tree. In a disposable HOME, the saved
+agents-infra binary must run `setup global --source-dir` against the source
+worktree derived from that same binary and then `verify global`, both with exit
+zero. An installed executable whose reported commit does not resolve in its
+repository blocks Step 0; the operator repairs or reinstalls a resolvable pair
+rather than substituting a nearby revision.
+
+In-flight runs: remain on their existing process images. Do not restart or
+kill them. Do not begin Step 2 while a run is at a handoff/acceptance mutation
+boundary.
+
+Mismatch window: none; this step only copies explicitly named non-secret
+artifacts and creates detached source worktrees.
+
+Rollback command: no installed artifact changes. On snapshot failure, stop and
+discard only the newly created task-scoped snapshot after inspecting it; no
+release step may start.
+
+Rollback status: **UNTESTED**.
+
+### Step 1 — hold agents-management at `v0.5.0`
+
+Repository/version: `skill-agents-management v0.5.0` at exact commit/checksum
+named above. This tag already exists; this task publishes nothing.
+
+Required board capability: the current installed board continues to spawn and
+route with embedded `v0.2.0`. The exact detached consumer candidate must pass:
+
+```bash
+cd "$PM_CANDIDATE/tools/board-cli"
+GOWORK=off go test -mod=mod ./internal/spawn -count=1
+GOWORK=off go build -mod=mod ./...
+```
+
+In-flight runs: unaffected; no executable changes.
+
+Mismatch window: none at runtime. A source-only scratch repin exists until its
+worktree is removed, but the installed board does not read it.
+
+Rollback commands for a failed scratch candidate:
+
+```bash
+cd "$PM_CANDIDATE/tools/board-cli"
+GOWORK=off go mod edit \
+  -require=github.com/relux-works/skill-agents-management@v0.2.0
+GOFLAGS=-mod=mod GOWORK=off go mod tidy
+GOWORK=off go test -mod=mod ./internal/spawn -count=1
+GOWORK=off go build -mod=mod ./...
